
// Generated by Melissa Bianco's Almighty Fingers ver 69 on Tues June 6 2006

// Faultline

// HISTORY

// War on Drugs

"WarOnDrugs5" "This plaque commemorates the death of Mina Horne, known to her comrades as the Illustrated Woman. She was killed when the Frost drug cartel, otherwise known as the Family, ambushed the Regulators as revenge for the brief imprisonment of Harry Frost. At her funeral, a shaken Back Alley Brawler said, 'Mina was a person who believed in the human spirit. We can best honor her by living the life she fought for.'"

// Superadine

"Superadine5" "In late 1982, the Back Alley Brawler and his Regulators were trying to mop up the last traces of Superadine on the city streets. A tip led them to this building, a front company for the Family. During the raid Harry Frost, the Family's leader, was accidentally killed, leaving his young son Sebastian to oversee his business. Since then, Superadine sales have inexplicably increased, despite the best efforts of the Brawler and his comrades."

// Hero Corps

"HeroCorps6" "In May of 1999, following the destruction of Hero Corps' original Paragon City headquarters, Countess Crey held a conference at this site to announce her sponsorship of the ill-fated company. She joined hands with Rebecca Foss, Hero Corps' founder, and said, 'A new world of heroes is upon us. Crey Technologies will not be left behind.'"

// Hero Corps

"HeroCorps7" "This plaque marks the site of the original Hero Corps headquarters, which was destroyed by a gang of mysterious soldiers clad in power armor. Although Hero Corps' employees made it out alive, Hero Corps was devastated by the loss. Its founder, Rebecca Foss, spoke tearfully the next morning on the radio program 'Wake Up Paragons.' 'Who could hate us so much?' she asked, 'We only want to help!'"

// In Memoriam

"InMemoriam" "This plaque, placed at the base of local artist Constance Brouwers' sculpture, serves as a memoriam to all the lives lost both during the recent dam flood and the blast at the hands of Faultline.  <p><p><p>Constance took great care to use original beams from the wreckage to create her work.  She said, 'I want to honor the lives we lost and show the remarkable rebirth of Overbrook.  We have a long way to go, but I have great hopes for this town.'"

